- Age of immediate older children: Having older children might reduce or increase stress depending on their age; newborn-focused stress might be less if older children are older or none.
- Relax/sleep when newborn is tended: Inability to relax or sleep when newborn is tended likely increases risk of postpartum depression.
- Mode of delivery: Caesarean section might be associated with higher risk due to physical recovery or complications.
- Relationship with husband: Poor or bad relationship likely increases risk of postpartum depression; good or friendly reduces risk.
- Birth compliancy: Complications during birth can increase risk of postpartum depression.
- Disease before pregnancy: Chronic disease or any disease before pregnancy may increase risk due to health burden.
- Abuse: Experience of abuse strongly increases risk of postpartum depression.
- Fear of pregnancy: Fear or anxiety related to pregnancy increases risk.
- Age of newborn: Very young newborns (0 to 6 months) might be associated with higher risk as postpartum depression usually manifests early.

